{
 "format": "mechanism-workshop",
 "version": 1,
 "id": "example-walker",
 "name": "Walking linkage robot",
 "description": "A six-legged walker built from three pairs of Jansen linkages on a three-throw crankshaft (throws 120° apart). One gear motor turns the crank; nothing else is driven.\n\nThis runs in free-body mode: the chassis is not grounded. The machine moves only because the feet push on the floor through unilateral contact with Coulomb friction. Watch \"Loaded feet\" and the locomotion summary in Diagnostics.\n\nTry it: the variants lower the floor friction to 0.08 (\"Icy floor\": the feet slide and the tow load wins), cut the motor torque limit (\"Weak motor\": it stalls) or raise the tow load; change a link length (for example the crank radius m) and see whether the linkage still assembles and walks. Nothing guarantees that an edited leg walks — diagnosing why not is the point.\n\nIdealisations: planar rigid bodies; pins are ideal revolute joints; part-to-part collisions are detected and reported but only floor contact produces forces.",
 "createdAt": "2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
 "modifiedAt": "2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
